Source format
-------------

debian/source/format is "3.0 (native)".

Omadungeon has no separate upstream: the Debian packaging lives in the game's
own git tree, is released in lockstep with it, and there is no upstream tarball
to diff against. A "3.0 (quilt)" package would mean inventing an orig tarball
that is byte-identical to the source and carrying an empty patch series, which
buys nothing and only adds a step to every release.

The one wart is that debian/changelog carries a Debian revision, 0.1.0-1, which
dpkg-source warns about for a native package ("native package version may not
have a revision"). The warning is not fatal and the revision is kept
deliberately, so that a packaging-only fix can be released as 0.1.0-2 without
retagging the game. If that warning ever needs to go away, drop the "-1" from
debian/changelog; nothing else in this packaging parses it.

debian/source/options excludes export/, dist/ and the .godot import cache from
the generated tarball. Without that, the tarball would carry over 300 MB of
exported binaries alongside the 160 MB of source audio it already has.


Build requirements
------------------

Building requires the Godot editor binary, version 4.7 or newer, on PATH or in
$GODOT_BIN. Godot 4 is not in the Debian archive, so it cannot appear in
Build-Depends without making dpkg-checkbuilddeps permanently unsatisfiable; it
is documented here and in the comment at the top of debian/control instead.

The build exports two presets from export_presets.cfg:

  "Linux x86_64"                   -> export/linux/omadungeon       (omadungeon)
  "Linux x86_64 (lite, no music)"  -> export/linux-lite/omadungeon  (omadungeon-lite)

Each is a complete copy of the Godot Linux export template with the game's PCK
embedded in it as an ELF section, which is why the binary packages are large
and why nothing in debian/rules may run strip over them.

Both presets use export_filter="all_resources", which packs Godot resources and
nothing else. Anything the game opens by path but that Godot does not import as
a resource - data/themes/fallback/colors.toml, the radio playlist's .lrc lyrics
- reaches the PCK only because include_filter names it, and both presets
exclude addons/, so the gdUnit4 test framework is not shipped. Leaving a
non-resource file out is invisible at export time: the export succeeds, the
game starts, and it exits 0 having drawn the whole screen magenta because every
colour role fell through to Color.MAGENTA. tools/deb.sh therefore refuses to
build a package until it has (a) found every such file in the binary's own PCK
directory and (b) run the game headless, in an empty HOME with no Omarchy and
no otter-shell theme, with no engine error on stderr. Add a new file of that
kind to include_filter in export_presets.cfg, and to required_pck_files() in
tools/deb.sh if a glob there does not already cover it.


Building without debhelper
--------------------------

    tools/deb.sh both

builds both .deb files into dist/ using nothing but dpkg-deb. It generates
DEBIAN/control from debian/control and debian/changelog, so the metadata has a
single source regardless of which path built the package. debian/rules falls
back to the same script when debhelper is not installed.


Dependencies
------------

The Godot export template links against the C library and nothing else; X11,
Wayland, EGL, GLES, ALSA and udev are all dlopen()ed at runtime. dpkg-shlibdeps
therefore reports libc6 and stops, which is why dh_shlibdeps is overridden to
do nothing and the Depends list is maintained by hand in debian/control.

    tools/check-deb-deps.sh            derive and print the list
    tools/check-deb-deps.sh --check    fail if debian/control has drifted

--check compares Depends, Recommends and Suggests, as sets, against both binary
stanzas: omadungeon and omadungeon-lite carry the same three lists and there is
nothing generating one from the other, so checking only the first would let an
edit to one of them through. Field order in debian/control is free; content is
not.

Re-run it after every Godot upgrade. It reports any SONAME it does not
recognise, which is how a newly added engine dependency gets noticed.
